abstract: "Microbes (Bacteria, Archaea, and Protists) play important roles in marine\
  \ food webs. They are primary producers and food for higher trophic levels. They\
  \ are carbon and nutrient cyclers, driving global biogeochemical cycles. The coastal\
  \ margin of British Columbia is experiencing multi-stressor events that have the\
  \ potential to greatly impact ecosystem function. Developing a climatology is essential\
  \ for uncovering the physical, chemical and biological drivers of marine microbial\
  \ community change in this region and elucidating community responses to increasingly\
  \ frequent ocean perturbations. \n\nFrom 2015-2025, The Hakai Institute Ocean Observing\
  \ Program routinely collected samples from multiple long-term oceanographic monitoring\
  \ stations to establish planktonic biodiversity baselines in the Northern Salish\
  \ Sea (weekly; Station QU39: 50.0307, -125.0992) and on the British Columbia Central\
  \ Coast (every 4-8 weeks; Station FZH01 (2016-2019): 51.6394, -127.8958; Station\
  \ KC10 (2019-2025): 51.650, -127.9516). As part of this effort, seawater environmental\
  \ DNA samples were collected from 5 depths spanning the entire water column (0,\
  \ 5, 30, 100 and 260/275/325 m respectively) to genetically characterize marine\
  \ microbes at an unprecedented temporal resolution. \n\nSpecifically, DNA was extracted\
  \ from these 0.2 um-filtered seawater samples and marker genes were amplified using\
  \ up to two different primer sets:\n\n1. The 515FY/926R universal three-domain rRNA\
  \ gene primers, simultaneously quantifying the relative abundance of amplicon sequencing\
  \ variants (ASVs) from bacteria 16S, archaea 16S, eukaryotic nuclear 18S, and eukaryotic\
  \ plastid 16S genes, and\n2. The V4F/V4RB eukaryote-specific primers, quantifying\
  \ the relative abundance of eukaryotic nuclear 18S ASVs only.\n\nAmplicon libraries\
  \ were sequenced on an Illumina MiSeq or NextSeq and processed using the open-source\
  \ bioinformatics software dada2, as implemented in QIIME2. These data constitute\
  \ a important biomolecular baseline for coastal British Columbia marine planktonic\
  \ biodiversity.\n\nThis data package contains links to the raw sequence data, wet-lab\
  \ protocols, and associated physical and chemical oceanographic data. It is freely\
  \ available to everyone, following the principles of equitable access and benefit\
  \ sharing. However, we expect all data users to give attribution to the data providers\
  \ (read our data license) and the use of these data should happen in the light of\
  \ fair use, i.e.: 1) respect the data providers, and provide helpful feedback on\
  \ data quality, and 2) communicate and/or collaborate with the providers if you\
  \ are considering using this dataset for manuscripts or other forms of reporting."
